<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Frideric Handel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1685–1759</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Georg Friedrich Händel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Frideric Handel was a German-born composer who spent most of his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Born in Halle, the son of a surgeon to the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1712, becoming a British subject in 1727. He wrote Italian operas, the Water Music and Music for the Royal Fireworks, and English oratorios such as Messiah (1742).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel.jpg" alt="George Frideric Handel" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Balthasar Denner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Frideric_Handel_by_Balthasar_Denner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
